# Coding pattern preferences

## Code Organization
- Keep the codebase clean and organized
- Avoid files exceeding 200-300 lines of code; refactor when approaching this limit
- Prefer simple solutions over complex ones
- Favor readability and maintainability

## Code Quality
- Avoid code duplication; check for existing implementations before writing new code
- Write environment-aware code that works properly in development, testing, and production
- Only make changes that are explicitly requested or clearly understood
- When fixing issues, first try to maintain the existing implementation pattern before introducing new technologies
- If introducing a new pattern, remove the old implementation to avoid duplicate logic

## Development Practices
- Never add stubbing or fake data patterns that affect development or production environments
- Only use mocking data for tests, never for development or production
- Avoid writing scripts directly in files, especially for one-time operations
- Never overwrite the .env file without explicit permission

## Change Management
- Be careful with changes that might affect other parts of the application
- Thoroughly test changes before submitting
- Document complex logic or non-obvious decisions
– Never overwrite my .env file without first asking and confirming
